<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kaifeng</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kaifeng is a city in north-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> plain. It was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Northern Song</a> and one of the largest and most commercialised cities of its time. It fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> Jin in 1127, ending the Northern Song period of the Song Dynasty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaifeng.jpg" alt="Kaifeng" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: caoyuan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E9%BE%99%E4%BA%AD%E5%85%AC%E5%9B%AD_-_panoramio_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 361 BCE King Hui of Wei moved the Wei capital to Yi and built the city of Daliang. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> conquered Wei, Daliang was destroyed apart from a mid-sized market town renamed Junyi. Under the Han it was the capital of the Kingdom of Liang, ruled by Liu Wu, who restored the walls and made it a centre of music, art and gardens. The nearby Qifeng was renamed Kaifeng to avoid the naming taboo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-jing-of-han">Emperor Jing of Han</a>. In the early 7th century, as Bianzhou, the city became a commercial hub when it was joined to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a>. During the Five Dynasties it was capital of four successive dynasties, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> made it their capital in 960 as Dongjing.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, Kaifeng became the largest and most prosperous city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, at the intersection of four major canals and surrounded by three rings of walls. It probably held between 600,000 and 700,000 people and is believed to have been the largest city in the world from 1013 to 1127. The memoir Dongjing Menghualu recalls its night and dawn markets, restaurants and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a>, and Zhang Zeduan's painting Along the River During the Qingming Festival depicts its bustle. The Iron Pagoda of Youguo Temple, built in 1049, is the oldest landmark of the city, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/su-song">Su Song</a>'s astronomical clock tower used an escapement two hundred years before they appeared in the clockworks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p>In 1127 the Jurchen Jin took the city and carried off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> imperial family and officials; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozong-of-song">Zhao Gou</a> escaped and the court never moved back. The Jin court itself moved to Kaifeng in 1214 to resist the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, and in 1233 the city fell to them after a ten-month siege. Kaifeng was vulnerable on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> floodplain: five major floods struck between 1375 and 1416. In 1642 the Ming army flooded the city during a siege by rebels, and only 30,000 of its 370,000 people remained.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kaifeng was rebuilt in 1662 and again after the flood of 1841, and was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> until 1954. It is one of the Eight Ancient Capitals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, having been the capital eight times, and has long been home to the Kaifeng Jews, the oldest Jewish community in China.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kaifeng” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1126–1127 Jin armies twice besieged the Song capital <b>Kaifeng</b>; in the Jingkang Incident of 1127 they took the city and carried off the retired emperor Huizong, the emperor Qinzong and thousands of members of the court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Yelü Deguang the army reached <b>Kaifeng</b>: in 946–947 he overthrew the Later Jin, whose second ruler had defied him, and briefly ruled north China before revolts forced him to withdraw; he died on the march north.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The dynasty's military weakness proved fatal when the Jurchen Jin captured the capital <b>Kaifeng</b> in 1127 and carried off the retired emperor Huizong and his son, the reigning emperor Qinzong.</blockquote><a href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Jin army crossed the North China Plain to <b>Kaifeng</b>, and in the Jingkang Incident it captured the capital, the retired Emperor Huizong, his successor Emperor Qinzong and most of the Imperial court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/southern-song">Southern Song · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…states one by one: Han in 230, Zhao in 228 (a rump state at Dai held out until 222), Wei in 225 after its capital <b>Daliang</b> was flooded, Chu in 223 after a first invasion under Li Xin had failed, Yan in 222 and Qi in 221 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Judaism's include Toledo, a great centre of Sephardic Jewry until the expulsion of 1492, and <b>Kaifeng</b>, whose synagogue was taken down around 1860.</blockquote><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move</a></li></ul></section>
